consiSt("ga",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Georgia","d":"Court of last resort. Hears direct appeals in cases within its constitutional jurisdiction (for example, constitutional questions raised and ruled on below, death-penalty cases, election contests), reviews Court of Appeals decisions by discretionary certiorari, answers certified questions from federal and other state courts, and regulates admission, discipline, and pro hac vice in its own court. Files through the mandatory SCED e-filing system."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Court of Appeals of Georgia","d":"Hears appeals and applications for appeal not within the Supreme Court’s jurisdiction; misfiled appeals and applications are transferred between the two courts. Sits in divisions of three judges, with en banc review by rule. Three terms a year (December, April, August); a case must be decided by the end of the term after the one to which it is docketed. Files through the mandatory eFast system."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Superior Courts","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts, organized by judicial circuit (one or more counties). Governed statewide by the Uniform Superior Court Rules (USCR); local rules were repealed and courts may add only standing orders that do not conflict, with actual notice to the parties."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"State Courts","d":"County-level courts of limited jurisdiction (civil cases and misdemeanors) in counties that have them. Governed by the Uniform State Court Rules, which largely track the USCR (the USCR text cross-refers to State Court Rules 6.2, 8.3, 36.1 and others where they differ)."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Georgia State-wide Business Court","d":"Statewide court for complex business cases with its own rules (posted by the Supreme Court as the State-wide Business Court Rules, June 11, 2026 version) and its own pro hac vice rule (Rule 4-4). Its rules were not collected."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Probate, Juvenile, Magistrate, and Municipal Courts","d":"Each class has its own uniform rules posted by the Supreme Court. Pro hac vice in magistrate court is under Uniform Magistrate Court Rule 7.5 and in probate court under Uniform Probate Court Rule 3.3.3 (per the State Bar’s pro hac vice page; those rule texts were not collected)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Georgia has an anti-SLAPP statute: O.C.G.A. § 9-11-11.1","d":"The official 2016 legislative summary describes Act 420 (HB 513), effective July 1, 2016, as revising the procedure for claims arising from acts that could be construed as in furtherance of the right of free speech, allowing a motion to strike, revising a definition, adding exceptions, and allowing a direct appeal from an order or judgment on such a claim or motion. It amended O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-34, 9-11-11.1, and 51-5-7.\nScope: the 2016 Act is widely reported to have broadened protection beyond petitioning activity to speech on an issue of public interest or concern; that point was not verified from collected text.\nThe code text itself could not be collected from a free official state source, so the elements, burden, and exceptions are not stated here.","c":"O.C.G.A. § 9-11-11.1; 2016 Ga. Laws Act 420 (HB 513) (Legislative Counsel summary)","v":false},{"t":"Procedure, deadlines, discovery stay, and fees: confirm in the code","d":"Not verified from collected text. Practitioners commonly describe § 9-11-11.1 as requiring the special motion to strike to be filed within a short period after service of the complaint, staying discovery while the motion is pending, requiring a prompt hearing, and awarding attorney’s fees to a prevailing moving defendant. Treat every one of those points (and any day count) as unconfirmed until checked against the current code text; later sessions (2017–2024) were not checked for amendments.","c":"O.C.G.A. § 9-11-11.1 (text not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Appeal: direct appeal of anti-SLAPP rulings","d":"Per the 2016 summary, the Act amended O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34 to allow a direct appeal of an order or judgment on an anti-SLAPP claim or motion, so no certificate of immediate review or interlocutory application should be needed. Confirm the current wording of § 5-6-34, which was amended again in 2025 (HB 176, SB 298, HB 155).","c":"O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34 (as amended by 2016 Act 420); 2025 Legislative Counsel summary","v":false}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice in superior, state, and magistrate courts: USCR 4.4 and a Georgia sponsor of record","d":"An eligible “Domestic Lawyer” (admitted elsewhere in the U.S., not disbarred or suspended anywhere) or “Foreign Lawyer” files a verified application with the court where the case is pending, serves it on all parties who have appeared and on the State Bar’s Office of General Counsel, and includes proof of service. The court may grant it summarily if no one objects; the Office of General Counsel or a party may object with a factual basis or ask for conditions.\nThe application must give the Appendix A information, including every Georgia pro hac vice application in the past two years and the name, address, phone, and bar number of an active Georgia lawyer who will sponsor the application and appear of record with the applicant. Foreign lawyers add an immigration-compliance affidavit.\nThe in-state lawyer stays responsible to the client and for conduct of the case. Out-of-state counsel’s conflicts do not delay any deadline, deposition, mediation, hearing, or trial.","c":"USCR 4.4 A–D, H & App. A","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice fees: $75 per application, $200 per year, January 15 deadline","d":"Superior court: a non-refundable $75 fee for each application, payable to the State Bar of Georgia when filed, plus an annual fee of $200 (no matter how many Georgia admissions) on first admission and by January 15 of each later year while admitted. A pro bono exemption applies to lawyers who charge no fee and work with a pro bono or nonprofit legal services program in a civil case, or for an indigent defendant in a criminal or habeas case.\nUnder the Rules of Professional Conduct, missing the January 15 annual payment brings a $100 late fee due by March 1; nonpayment can lead to discipline and unauthorized-practice exposure.\nThe State Bar’s page says mailed submissions are $275 or $75 (that is, first-time and repeat applicants) and that the Bar reviews pro hac vice for magistrate, state, and superior courts, the State Board of Workers’ Compensation, the State-wide Business Court, and the Georgia Tax Court.","c":"USCR 4.4 E.2–E.4; Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5 (final paragraph on pro hac vice fees); gabar.org pro hac vice page","v":true},{"t":"Grounds to deny: “regular practice” and prior appearances without approval","d":"Applications ordinarily should be granted unless admission may harm the administration of justice or other parties’ interests, the client may be inadequately represented, the applicant appears so often that it amounts to regular practice in Georgia, or the applicant previously filed or appeared in a Georgia court without approval under the USCR. Admission may be revoked on the same grounds. Eligibility also requires that the lawyer not reside or regularly work at an office in Georgia (with exceptions for in-house lawyers and Georgia residents who practice from offices elsewhere and only temporarily here).","c":"USCR 4.4 A.2, D.3–D.4, G","v":true},{"t":"Supreme Court of Georgia: apply to the State Bar first, with local counsel","d":"A non-resident lawyer admitted in the highest court of another state, territory, or D.C. must first file a pro hac vice application with the State Bar. An application must also be filed in the Supreme Court (if the Bar has not yet approved, contemporaneously with the first filing). It must be served on all parties and include a certificate of good standing from every admitting jurisdiction issued no more than 30 days before filing, contact details, the parties represented, any denial, revocation, discipline, or sanction history, and the number of Georgia pro hac vice admissions in the calendar year with case details. Attach an affidavit and the Bar’s acknowledgment of submission and payment (or proof of submission, supplemented within one business day after the acknowledgment arrives).\nThe application must be made by a member of the Supreme Court’s bar, who serves as local counsel of record and must actively participate. A pro bono waiver is available for indigent clients.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 4(9)(a)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Court of Appeals: separate application and a $200 check to the Georgia Bar Foundation","d":"An out-of-state lawyer must file a pro hac vice application before appearing in a single case, served on all parties, with a current certificate of good standing from the home jurisdiction’s highest court, business address, email, phone, and the party represented. The application may be made by the lawyer or by a member of the Court of Appeals bar. Each application requires a $200 check payable to “IOLTA/Georgia Bar Foundation,” mailed to the Foundation, and a certification that it was sent. Fee waiver is available for pro bono representation of an indigent party. Admitted lawyers must register in eFast.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 9(c)(1)–(5)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances must be filed as a “Limited Appearance”","d":"No attorney may undertake limited representation in superior court without first filing a separate entry titled “Limited Appearance” that complies with USCR 4.2 and states the limited purpose and duration. Within 5 days after the limited work ends, file a notice that it has ended (with the client’s last known mailing and email address) and serve the client, opposing counsel or parties, and the assigned judge. Without a proper limited-appearance notice, the lawyer stays counsel of record until a USCR 4.3 withdrawal is granted.","c":"USCR 4.13","v":true},{"t":"Entry of appearance: bar number and State-Bar-registered email on every pleading","d":"No attorney may appear in superior court until filing a signed entry of appearance or signed pleading stating the case style and number, the party represented, and the attorney’s name, State Bar number, office address, phone, fax, and the email address registered with the State Bar of Georgia. Within 48 hours after being retained, mail to the court and opposing counsel or file the entry of appearance (late filing does not bar the appearance). Out-of-state lawyers without a Georgia bar number appear through USCR 4.4.","c":"USCR 4.2","v":true},{"t":"E-filing access","d":"Superior courts: e-filing is available when required by law and otherwise where adopted under Judicial Council standards; registered filers who opt into electronic service are deemed served on filing; a filing is presumed filed when the e-filing service provider receives it and confirms receipt. Statewide mandatory superior-court e-filing is statutory (O.C.G.A. § 15-6-11, not collected).\nAppellate courts: e-filing is mandatory for attorneys in the Court of Appeals (eFast) and the Supreme Court (SCED). Lawyers admitted pro hac vice receive e-filing access and must register.","c":"USCR 36.16; Ga. Ct. App. R. 9(c)(5), 46; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 4(9)(e), 4(12), 13(1)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission (Rule 5.5)","d":"A Domestic Lawyer not disbarred or suspended may provide temporary services in Georgia in association with an actively participating Georgia lawyer, in connection with a proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear, in connection with arbitration or mediation related to the home practice (unless the forum requires pro hac vice), or otherwise arising from the home practice. In-house work for an employer and services authorized by federal or Georgia law are also allowed. No office or systematic and continuous presence and no holding out as Georgia-admitted. Foreign lawyers have a parallel, narrower set of permissions.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(b)–(f)","v":true},{"t":"Full admission: on motion (reciprocity) or the Attorneys’ Examination","d":"Admission on motion without exam requires, among other things, an ABA-approved J.D. or LL.B., admission by exam in a U.S. jurisdiction that has reciprocity with Georgia (with the stricter rules of that jurisdiction applied to its applicants), active practice for five of the seven years before applying, good standing everywhere admitted, no prior Georgia exam failure or fitness denial, and fitness certification. Fees: $1,200 fitness application plus $1,300 petition.\nOtherwise, a lawyer admitted by exam elsewhere may take the Attorneys’ Examination (essay portion and MPT, no MBE; passing score 135), also with a $1,200 fitness fee. These rules are dated November 1, 2022; confirm current fees.","c":"Ga. Bar Admission Rules Part C §§ 1–4; Part D §§ 1–5","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Georgia Rules of Professional Conduct, Bar Rule 4-102(d), each with a stated maximum penalty","d":"Georgia’s rules are published as part of Bar Rule 4-102 and follow the ABA Model Rules numbering, but each rule ends with its own maximum penalty (for example, disbarment for Rule 1.6, public reprimand for Rule 1.5). Levels of discipline run from formal letter of admonition and confidential reprimand to State Disciplinary Review Board reprimand, public reprimand (administered by a superior court judge in open court), suspension, and disbarment.","c":"Ga. Bar R. 4-102; Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5, 1.6","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality is broader, and disclosure exceptions differ (Rule 1.6)","d":"Rule 1.6(a) protects “all information gained in the professional relationship,” expressly including information the client asked to keep private or whose disclosure would be embarrassing or likely detrimental. Permissive disclosure covers preventing harm or substantial financial loss from client or third-party criminal conduct, and serious injury or death. If the client has already acted, disclosure is allowed only if the harm has not yet occurred, and the lawyer must first try, if feasible, to persuade the client not to act or to warn the victim. Maximum penalty: disbarment.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(a)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"No screening cure for lateral hires (Rule 1.10)","d":"Georgia Rule 1.10(a) imputes a lawyer’s conflicts under Rules 1.7, 1.8(c), 1.9, and 2.2 to everyone in the firm, with no paragraph allowing a screen to avoid imputation of a lateral lawyer’s former-client conflict (unlike ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2)). The only listed cure is client waiver under Rule 1.7.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 1.10(a)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Prohibited transactions: related-lawyer rule kept; no humanitarian-aid exception","d":"Rule 1.8(e) allows advancing costs (repayment contingent on outcome) or paying costs for an indigent client, and nothing else; there is no exception for modest living expenses for pro bono clients. Rule 1.8(i) still bars a lawyer from representing a client directly adverse to someone the lawyer knows is represented by the lawyer’s parent, grandparent, child, grandchild, sibling, or spouse without informed consent (personal, not imputed). Rule 1.8(h) allows a malpractice-arbitration agreement only with informed written consent signed by the client.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(e), (h), (i)","v":true},{"t":"Fee arbitration clauses must be separately initialed (Rule 1.5(b))","d":"A lawyer may include an agreement to arbitrate fee disputes only with the client’s informed consent in a writing signed by the client; the clause and the lawyer’s disclosures must be a separate paragraph, in a font at least as large as the rest of the contract, initialed by both client and lawyer. Contingent-fee agreements must be in writing and say whether expenses come out before or after the fee; at the end the lawyer must give a written statement of the outcome and, if there is a recovery, the remittance, method, fee, and any split with another firm.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 3.4 differs from the ABA text","d":"Georgia reserves the slots for ABA Rule 3.4(c) (knowingly disobeying a tribunal rule), (d) (frivolous discovery), and (e) (improper trial references). It adds a ban on using evidence-gathering methods that violate the opposing party’s or counsel’s legal rights, and on presenting or threatening criminal charges solely to gain an advantage in a civil matter. Maximum penalty: disbarment.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 3.4","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct: felony conviction is itself a violation; no ABA 8.4(d) or (g) paragraph","d":"Rule 8.4(a) makes it misconduct to be convicted of a felony, or of a moral-turpitude misdemeanor related to fitness; to fail to pay within ten days a final judgment for money the lawyer collected as a lawyer; and to commit a fitness-related criminal act the lawyer has admitted in judicio. First-offender probation and nolo pleas count as convictions. The Georgia text has no paragraph on conduct prejudicial to the administration of justice or on harassment and discrimination.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(a)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Reporting another lawyer’s misconduct is only a “should” (Rule 8.3)","d":"A lawyer who knows of another lawyer’s violation raising a substantial question about honesty or fitness “should” inform the appropriate authority, and the rule states that there is no disciplinary penalty for violating it.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 8.3","v":true},{"t":"Self-reporting within 60 days (Rule 9.1)","d":"Georgia lawyers must notify the State Bar within 60 days of admission in another jurisdiction, of any felony or fitness-related moral-turpitude misdemeanor conviction, and of discipline imposed by any other jurisdiction (state, federal, territorial, or foreign). Maximum penalty: public reprimand.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 9.1","v":true},{"t":"Solicitation: 30-day bar on written accident solicitation (Rule 7.3)","d":"Written solicitation about personal injury, wrongful death, or an accident or disaster involving the recipient or a relative is barred unless the accident or disaster happened more than 30 days earlier. Live person-to-person solicitation for pecuniary gain is barred except to lawyers, family, close personal or prior professional contacts, and routine business users of the services.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 7.3(b)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Willful blindness counts (Rule 1.2(d)); trust accounts pay IOLTA interest to the Georgia Bar Foundation","d":"A lawyer may not “knowingly or with willful blindness” counsel or assist client crime or fraud. Nominal or short-term client funds go in an IOLTA account at an approved institution, with interest remitted at least quarterly to the Georgia Bar Foundation; other client funds go in an interest-bearing account for the client’s benefit.","c":"Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(d); 1.15(II)","v":true}],"style":"State of Georgia","home":"https://www.gasupreme.us/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Discovery must be finished within 6 months of the answer to use the court’s compulsory process","d":"Under USCR 5.1, discovery must be started promptly and completed within 6 months after the answer is filed (or 6 months starting 30 days after service if no timely answer) for a party to use the court’s compulsory process to compel it. The court may open, extend, reopen, or shorten the period, but ask before it closes. Motions to compel must quote or attach each request and response and certify a good-faith conference.","c":"USCR 5.1, 6.4"},{"t":"Responses to motions are due in 30 days, and there is no hearing unless you ask","d":"Responses are due 30 days after service of a motion (not 14). Civil motions, including summary judgment, are decided without oral hearing unless the court orders otherwise; for summary judgment, a separate “Request for Oral Hearing” must be filed with the motion or within 5 days after the response time. Summary-judgment motions need a separate statement of material facts, and the response needs its own statement of disputed facts.","c":"USCR 6.2, 6.3, 6.5"},{"t":"Email the judge when you e-file a motion","d":"When a motion or response is e-filed, the filer must notify the opposing parties and the assigned judge (or the judge’s designee) by email contemporaneously and no later than 24 hours after e-filing. Every pre-trial motion must also include citations of authority and, for unstipulated facts, affidavits or record citations.","c":"USCR 6.1"},{"t":"Recusal motions: 5 days from learning, 10 days before the hearing","d":"A superior court recusal motion must be filed and presented within 5 days after learning the grounds and at least 10 days before the hearing or trial, with a specific affidavit. Conclusory affidavits fail, and the motion cannot delay the proceeding.","c":"USCR 25.1–25.2"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeal needs a trial-court certificate and a 10-day application, with no extensions","d":"Most non-final orders reach the appellate courts only by application after the trial court signs a certificate of immediate review. The application is due within 10 days after the certificate is entered (filed with the clerk). Neither appellate court grants extensions for interlocutory applications or responses, and if granted, a notice of appeal must be filed in the trial court within 10 days of the grant order. The statutory time to obtain the certificate is in O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34(b) (not collected).","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 16(d), 30(a), (h), (j); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30"},{"t":"Some final judgments are appealable only by discretionary application","d":"For case types listed in O.C.G.A. § 5-6-35 (statute not collected; divorce decrees are one example the rules mention), the appeal is by application filed in the appellate court within 30 days after the order is entered (7 days for dispossessory orders under O.C.G.A. § 44-7-56). An extension is possible only by motion filed on or before the due date. If granted, file the notice of appeal within 10 days.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 31(a), (b), (h), (j); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 33–34"},{"t":"Court of Appeals reconsideration motions are due by 4:30 p.m., not midnight","d":"Most e-filings in the Court of Appeals count as filed through 11:59 p.m., but a motion for reconsideration must be received by 4:30 p.m. within 10 days after the judgment; later filings count as received the next business day. Extensions only for providential cause by motion before the 10 days run. Mail-box and prison-mailbox rules do not apply.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 4(b)–(d), 37(b), 46(a)"},{"t":"Certiorari takes two filings in two courts","d":"To seek Supreme Court review of a Court of Appeals decision, first file a notice of intent to apply for certiorari in the Court of Appeals within 10 days after judgment (or after the ruling on reconsideration), then file the petition in the Supreme Court within 20 days after judgment or the reconsideration ruling, and file notice of the petition in the Court of Appeals the same day. A motion for permission to file a second reconsideration motion does not toll the 10 days.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 37(d), 38(a); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 38"},{"t":"Leaves of absence work in trial courts but not in the appellate courts","d":"In superior court, a lawyer can protect cases with a USCR 16.1 notice filed at least 30 days before a leave of 30 cumulative days or less; it stands granted unless opposing counsel objects or the court denies it within ten days. Longer leaves need a USCR 16.2 application. A leave never extends a deadline set by law or the court. The Court of Appeals and the Supreme Court do not recognize leaves of absence at all.","c":"USCR 16.1–16.3; Ga. Ct. App. R. 9(f); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 4(10)"},{"t":"Withdrawal requires a 20-day client notice with prescribed contents","d":"To withdraw in superior court, give the client written notice with the items listed in USCR 4.3(2) (including the client’s right to object within 20 days and the exact 20th day), file a notification certificate, and then request an order. The request is granted as of course only if made before a pretrial order is entered; the judge may refuse it if it would delay a trial on a published calendar, disrupt the court, or be manifestly unfair to the client. Substitution of counsel can instead be done by a notice signed by the party and the new lawyer.","c":"USCR 4.3(1)–(3)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: appearing before approval can sink later applications","d":"A court should deny pro hac vice if the applicant previously filed or appeared in a Georgia court without approval under the USCR, and frequent appearances can be treated as regular practice. The annual $200 fee is due January 15 for every year you remain admitted in any Georgia case.","c":"USCR 4.4 D.3(d)–(e), E.3; Ga. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5 (pro hac vice fee paragraph)"},{"t":"Default judgments need a written service certificate","d":"A party seeking default judgment must attach to the proposed judgment a written certificate stating the date and type of service, whether proof of service was filed within 5 business days of service (or when it was filed), that no defensive pleading appears of record, and the defendant’s military status if required.","c":"USCR 15"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory strike of a superior court judge; recusal motion within 5 days with affidavit","d":"The collected rules provide no peremptory substitution of judge. A motion to recuse or disqualify must be in writing with affidavits fully stating the facts, filed and presented no later than 5 days after the affiant first learned of the grounds and no later than 10 days before the hearing or trial, unless good cause is shown; it may not delay the proceeding. The affidavit must be definite and specific as to time, place, persons, and circumstances of extra-judicial conduct; bare conclusions are insufficient.\nThe challenged judge immediately stops acting on the merits and decides only timeliness and legal sufficiency, assuming the facts true. If the motion passes, another judge (chosen by the USCR 25.4 method, never by the challenged judge) hears it; the allegations stand denied automatically and the challenged judge may not otherwise oppose it. The challenged judge applies Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11.","c":"USCR 25.1–25.7","v":true},{"t":"Recusal in the appellate courts","d":"Court of Appeals: motion with affidavits within 20 days of docketing (absent good cause), decided on the papers by the other two judges of the division plus the presiding judge of the next division. Supreme Court: motion with affidavits no later than 5 days after learning of the grounds and 10 days before any oral argument; the challenged Justice decides, alone or after consulting the others.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 44(a)–(e); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 26(2)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Cameras and recording in trial courts: a presumption of approval","d":"Anyone (including media) seeking to record a superior court proceeding submits a request, at least 24 hours ahead where practicable. The court notifies the parties; a properly submitted request should generally be approved, and the judge may deny or limit it only after specific findings on listed factors. Parties, witnesses, and alleged victims waive objections not made in writing or on the record before the proceeding.","c":"USCR 22(F)–(G)","v":true},{"t":"Sealing court files requires a finding that privacy harm clearly outweighs the public interest","d":"All court records are public unless limited by law or the USCR 21 procedure. An order limiting access must specify what is limited, for how long, and why, and may issue only on a finding that harm to a person’s privacy clearly outweighs the public interest. Any party or nonparty opposing may demand a hearing within 30 days. Review is by interlocutory application to the appellate court.","c":"USCR 21, 21.1–21.5 (as amended Nov. 5, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Constitutional speech protections: not addressed in collected text","d":"Whether the Georgia Constitution’s speech clause is read more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked; no constitutional or case text was collected.","c":"Ga. Const. (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Uniform rules, no local rules","d":"All local rules expired; the USCR apply statewide and are not subject to local deviation. A judge may supersede a specific rule in a specific case only by an order explaining the necessity, served on the attorneys. Courts may adopt standing orders on matters the USCR do not address (for example, ADR, e-filing, registry payments), but only with actual notice to the parties; posting in the courthouse is not enough.","c":"USCR 1.2(E)–(F), 1.4, 1.5","v":true},{"t":"Civil Practice Act is statutory and recently amended","d":"Georgia’s rules of civil procedure are the Civil Practice Act, O.C.G.A. § 9-11-1 et seq., enacted by the General Assembly rather than adopted by the court. Official legislative summaries show 2025 amendments to §§ 9-11-12 and 9-11-41 (SB 68, effective April 21, 2025, on timing and procedure for answers, discovery, and dismissals) and § 9-11-26 (SB 69, litigation-funding discovery), and 2026 amendments to §§ 9-11-28 and 9-11-30 (HB 1208, depositions before certified court reporters, effective July 1, 2026). The code text was not collected.","c":"O.C.G.A. § 9-11-1 et seq.; 2025 and 2026 Legislative Counsel summaries","v":false}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Rules of the Court of Appeals of Georgia (complete rules effective March 26, 2026, gaappeals.gov); Rules of the Supreme Court of Georgia (effective Jan. 1, 2024, as amended through July 2, 2026, gasupreme.us); Uniform Superior Court Rules (compilation dated Nov. 5, 2026) Rules 21.4, 38, 41. The Appellate Practice Act (O.C.G.A. § 5-6-30 et seq.) is statutory and its text could not be collected from a free official state source; statements that depend only on the statute are flagged “statute not collected.” Legislative Counsel summaries for 2016 and 2025 (legis.ga.gov) were used for recent amendments.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Georgia: direct appeal, or application first?","d":"Georgia has two appellate courts with divided jurisdiction: the Supreme Court of Georgia and the Court of Appeals of Georgia. Every appellant’s brief and every application must open with a jurisdictional statement explaining why this court (and not the other) has jurisdiction and why the appeal is timely; misfiled cases are transferred.\nThe first question is always whether you may file a notice of appeal in the trial court or must first apply to the appellate court for permission. Interlocutory orders generally require a trial-court certificate of immediate review plus an application filed within 10 days; certain final judgments listed in O.C.G.A. § 5-6-35 require a discretionary application within 30 days. Application deadlines are strict and, for interlocutory applications, never extended.\nBoth courts require attorneys to e-file (Court of Appeals: eFast; Supreme Court: SCED), and e-filing does not serve anyone: serve separately and certify it.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 11(b), 25(a)(3), 30, 31, 46; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 13(1), 14, 19(1)(c), 30, 33"},"groups":[["Which court",[{"t":"Jurisdictional statement required in both courts","d":"An appellant’s brief must state (i) the basis for appellate jurisdiction (for example, O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-34, 5-6-35), (ii) why this court rather than the other has jurisdiction (Ga. Const. Art. VI, Sec. VI, Pars. II–III; O.C.G.A. § 15-3-3.1), and (iii) the filing dates showing timeliness (O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-38, 5-6-39). In the Supreme Court, a party claiming jurisdiction based on a constitutional question must cite where in the record the question was raised and ruled on below.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 25(a)(3); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 19(1)(c)"},{"t":"Transfers between the courts","d":"An appeal or application filed in the wrong court is transferred by order. In the Supreme Court a transferred case proceeds as if originally filed there; parties may elect within two business days of docketing to rely on briefs already filed in the Court of Appeals, and must renew any oral-argument request within five days. No second filing fee if it was paid in the Court of Appeals.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 11(b)–(c), 32; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 5(1)(d), 10(4), 51(4)"},{"t":"Election contests in the Supreme Court","d":"Emergency motions, motions to expedite, and applications in election-contest cases must include the basis for the challenge, a jurisdictional statement (citing, e.g., O.C.G.A. §§ 21-2-5(e), 21-2-528 for timeliness), an enumeration of errors, the steps taken to expedite the case below, and a standing explanation.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 29"}]],["Starting a direct appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal in the trial court (statute)","d":"A direct appeal starts with a notice of appeal filed in the trial court under O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-37 and 5-6-38. The statutory filing period (commonly 30 days after entry of judgment, extended by a timely motion for new trial or similar motion) was not verified from collected text. The notice must show service on all opposing counsel or parties by mail, personal, or electronic service, with names and full mailing addresses. The appeal is docketed only after the notice, record, and any requested transcripts reach the appellate clerk.","c":"O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-37, 5-6-38 (statute not collected); Ga. Ct. App. R. 6(b)(1), 11(a)"},{"t":"One extension of the notice-of-appeal deadline, requested before it runs","d":"Under O.C.G.A. § 5-6-39, only one extension may be granted, it cannot exceed the original period, and the request must be made before the original period expires. A request may be filed in the Court of Appeals only after a bona fide effort to get the extension from the trial court, explaining why it could not be obtained, with a stamped “filed” copy of the order and the filing fee.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 16(a)"},{"t":"Transcript costs and record transmission","d":"An attorney who files a motion for new trial or a notice of appeal designating a transcript is personally responsible for paying the court reporter, and the filing certifies that the transcript has been ordered. The trial clerk compiles and transmits the record under O.C.G.A. § 5-6-43. Original evidence is not sent: in the Court of Appeals, move that court for an order; in the Supreme Court, include a description or photograph in the transcript and, if originals are essential, ask the trial court first and the Supreme Court only if that fails. Audio and video exhibits must travel with the transcript, and the appellant bears the burden of a complete record.","c":"USCR 41.3–41.4; Ga. Ct. App. R. 18(b), 21; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 67–73"},{"t":"Objections to the record must be made below","d":"The appellee waives objections to the appellant’s failure to comply with the Appellate Practice Act on filing the transcript or transmitting the record unless the objection was made and ruled on in the trial court before transmittal.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 20; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 74"}]],["Interlocutory appeals (certificate plus application)",[{"t":"10 days after the certificate of immediate review, no extensions","d":"The application must be filed in the appellate court within 10 days after entry (filing with the clerk) of the trial court’s order granting the certificate of immediate review. It must include a jurisdictional statement and stamped “filed” copies of the order and the certificate, both bearing the judge’s actual signature (no conformed or stamped signatures unless that court’s official practice is electronic signing). No extension is available for the application or any response.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 16(d), 30(a), (c), (h); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30"},{"t":"Standard for granting","d":"Granted only when the issue appears dispositive, the order appears erroneous and will probably cause substantial error at trial (Court of Appeals adds: or will adversely affect the appellant’s rights until final judgment, in which case the appeal is expedited), or precedent is desirable. The applicant bears the burden.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(b); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 31"},{"t":"What to attach; 100-page exhibit cap in the Court of Appeals","d":"Attach the motion that produced the order and any responses, and enough of the record to show the issues in context; insufficient material means denial. In the Court of Appeals, e-filed exhibits are capped at 100 pages total (excluding the brief, index, order, certificate, the underlying motion and responses, and transcripts) unless counsel files a signed good-faith certificate; unnecessary exhibits can draw a sanction up to $10,000.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(e)–(g); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30"},{"t":"Responses, grant, and notice of appeal","d":"Responses are due within 10 days of docketing (encouraged, not required unless ordered). If the application is granted, file a notice of appeal in the trial court within 10 days of the grant order. No pleadings are accepted more than 30 days after the order granting, denying, or dismissing the application or the reconsideration motion.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(i)–(k); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30"},{"t":"Direct appeals for anti-SLAPP and certain immunity rulings","d":"Official summaries say O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34 was amended in 2016 to allow a direct appeal of orders on anti-SLAPP claims or motions, and in 2025 (SB 298, effective July 1, 2025) to provide for direct appeals relating to the grant, denial, or refusal to rule on certain immunities and privileges. The amended statute was not collected; check whether your order qualifies before relying on a direct appeal.","c":"O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34 (statute not collected); 2016 Act 420; 2025 Act 298 (Legislative Counsel summaries)"}]],["Discretionary applications",[{"t":"30 days from entry; 7 days for dispossessory","d":"For final judgments in case types covered by O.C.G.A. § 5-6-35, file an application in the appellate court within 30 days after entry of the order (7 days for dispossessory orders under O.C.G.A. § 44-7-56). Include a jurisdictional statement, enumeration of errors, and a stamped “filed” copy of the order with the judge’s actual signature. An extension is possible only by a Rule 40(b) motion filed on or before the due date (separate fee); no extension for responses.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 31(a), (c), (h); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 33"},{"t":"Standards for granting","d":"Court of Appeals: reversible error appears to exist; precedent is desirable; further development of the common law (particularly in divorce cases) is desirable; or the application seeks review of a final, timely divorce decree with possible merit (the last two grounds need a counsel certificate of good faith, and a frivolous application can bring a sanction up to $10,000). Supreme Court: reversible error appears to exist, or precedent is desirable.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 31(b); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 34"},{"t":"Supersedeas effect, response, and notice of appeal","d":"Filing a discretionary application acts as supersedeas to the same extent as a notice of appeal (O.C.G.A. § 5-6-35(h)). Responses are due within 10 days of docketing. If granted, file the notice of appeal within 10 days of the grant order. The same 100-page exhibit cap and 30-day late-filing bar apply as for interlocutory applications.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 31(g), (i)–(k), 40(a); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 33"},{"t":"2025 amendments","d":"Official summaries report 2025 amendments to §§ 5-6-34 and 5-6-35 (HB 176: direct appeals from guilty pleas now by application, and out-of-time motion for new trial or notice of appeal procedure in new § 5-6-39.1; HB 155: appellate procedures for zoning and quasi-judicial land-use decisions, Part I effective Jan. 1, 2026). Statute text not collected.","c":"2025 Acts 252 (HB 176), 128 (HB 155) (Legislative Counsel summary)"}]],["Briefing schedule",[{"t":"Court of Appeals: 20 / 40 / 20","d":"Appellant’s brief and enumeration of errors: within 20 days after docketing (failure can mean dismissal and contempt). Appellee: within 40 days after docketing or 20 days after the appellant’s brief, whichever is later; optional except for the State in criminal cases. Reply: within 20 days after the appellee’s brief; only one reply. “Other parties” may file one brief by the appellee’s deadline. Extension motions must be filed before the due date; only written orders count.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 16(b), 23(a)–(d)"},{"t":"Supreme Court: 20 / 40 / 50, including cross-appeals","d":"Appellant’s principal brief within 20 days after docketing; appellee within 40 days after docketing or 20 days after the appellant’s brief, whichever is later; reply within 50 days after docketing or 10 days after the response, whichever is later. Cross-appeals run on the same schedule from the cross-appeal’s docketing. After certiorari is granted, the same schedule runs from the grant order and briefs address only the questions the Court posed. Count from the day after docketing, including weekends and holidays; deadlines falling on a closed day roll to the next business day.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 10(1)–(3), 11, 45"},{"t":"Amicus briefs","d":"Without leave: within 10 days after the supported party’s brief is due (supporting neither party: 10 days after the response or reply is due); for applications in the Court of Appeals, within 5 days after the supported party’s application or response is due. Later only with leave, by motion attaching the brief. Amici cannot move for reconsideration. Supreme Court amicus briefs are limited to 7,000 words.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 26; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 20(5), 23"},{"t":"No letter briefs; supplemental briefs only with leave","d":"New authority after briefing must come in as a supplemental brief on motion with the proposed brief attached. In the Supreme Court, a granted supplemental brief is due within 5 days of approval and any response within 5 days after it is filed. Supplemental briefs cannot add enumerations of error.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 27; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 24"}]],["Length, format, and content",[{"t":"Court of Appeals limits","d":"Principal briefs: 8,400 words (civil) or 14,000 words (criminal). Reply briefs, supplemental briefs, emergency motions, motions for reconsideration and responses, and other motions: 4,200 words. Paper filers: 30 pages civil, 50 criminal, 15 for replies and motions. Include the certificate “This submission does not exceed the word count limit imposed by Rule 24.” Format: 14-point serif proportional font, double-spaced, 1-inch margins, no attachments to briefs or motions.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 2(c), 24(c), (f)–(g), 41(b)"},{"t":"Supreme Court limits","d":"Principal briefs: 10,000 words; reply briefs: 7,000; petitions for certiorari, applications, motions, and responses: 7,000; motions for reconsideration: 5,000; death-penalty principal briefs: 20,000. Paper limits are 30, 20, 20, and 10 pages respectively. Certify “This submission does not exceed the word-count limit imposed by Rule 20.” Format: serif font no smaller than 13-point Century Schoolbook (footnotes 11-point), 1.2x to double spacing, 1-inch margins, no hyperlinks to outside documents. Noncompliant documents may be struck without leave to refile.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 16, 17, 20, 27(1)"},{"t":"Enumerations not argued are abandoned","d":"Each enumerated error must be supported by argument, citation of authority, and specific citation to the record (electronic record cites as volume and PDF page, e.g., V2-46); otherwise it may be deemed abandoned and the court will not search the record. Georgia cases must be cited to the official reporters with volume and page.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 24(d), 25(d); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 22"},{"t":"Certificate of Interested Persons in the Supreme Court","d":"With the first filing (including an extension motion), the appellant, applicant, petitioner, or movant must file a separate Certificate of Interested Persons listing every party ever named, every attorney who appeared below, and every witness called at any trial or hearing. The appellee must supplement it.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 26.1"}]],["Oral argument",[{"t":"Court of Appeals","d":"Argument is only by grant of a request filed as a motion within 20 days after docketing (a briefing extension does not extend it), stating with particularity how argument will aid decision and the other side’s position. 15 minutes per side. No argument on applications or motions. Remote appearance by request, ideally at least 7 days before argument.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 28(a), (d)"},{"t":"Supreme Court","d":"Mandatory in granted certiorari, death-penalty, interim-review, and certified-question cases; otherwise by request filed within 20 days after docketing (appellee: within 10 days after the appellant’s brief), up to 3,000 words, stating specific reasons. 20 minutes per side (30 in death-penalty appeals); the appellant may reserve up to 5 minutes for rebuttal by notice two weeks ahead. Remote argument only in exceptional circumstances, and no hybrid arguments.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 50(2)–(3), 51, 53(2), 54(1)"}]],["Reconsideration (rehearing)",[{"t":"Court of Appeals: 10 days, by 4:30 p.m.","d":"File within 10 days from the judgment or dismissal, received by 4:30 p.m. (later filings count as the next business day). Granted only if the Court overlooked a material fact, statute, or controlling decision requiring a different result, or misapplied the law. No second motion without permission; no oral argument; 4,200 words.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 37(a)–(e), (h)"},{"t":"Supreme Court: 10 days","d":"Must be received or e-filed within 10 days from the ruling, attaching the ruling; no prison-mailbox rule. 5,000 words. No second motion without permission. A motion to stay the remittitur to seek U.S. Supreme Court review must be filed with the reconsideration motion or within the time to file one.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 13(3), 27, 61"}]],["Review in the Supreme Court of Georgia (certiorari)",[{"t":"Notice of intent, then petition","d":"File a notice of intent to apply for certiorari in the Court of Appeals within 10 days after judgment or the ruling on reconsideration (reconsideration is not a prerequisite). File the petition in the Supreme Court within 20 days after judgment or the reconsideration ruling, with the filing fee and a copy of the Court of Appeals decision and any reconsideration order, and file notice of the petition in the Court of Appeals the same day.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 38(a); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 38, 39"},{"t":"Standard and form","d":"Granted only in cases of great concern, gravity, or importance to the public, such as conflicts in the case law, precedent that warrants reconsideration, or an important recurring question of state law; generally not to correct error in factual findings or the application of a correct rule. Questions presented go first, before the tables. Considered without oral argument. Responses within 20 days (failure to respond is treated as an acknowledgment that the standard is met, though not binding); replies within 10 days; no sur-replies.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 40–42.1"},{"t":"Remittitur","d":"Court of Appeals: remittitur goes to the trial court after 10 days from judgment unless reconsideration or a notice of intent to seek certiorari is filed. Supreme Court: after 10 days following judgment or denial of reconsideration. The trial clerk files the remittitur with the original action.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 39; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 60; USCR 38"}]],["Stays, emergencies, and sanctions",[{"t":"Supersedeas","d":"In civil cases, a notice of appeal serves as supersedeas once the appellant pays all trial-court costs; on the appellee’s motion, the trial court may require a supersedeas bond. In the Supreme Court, a supersedeas motion must say whether a stay was sought in the trial court, include the order and stamped notice of appeal, and explain the urgency.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 40(a); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 9"},{"t":"Emergency motions","d":"Used sparingly to preserve jurisdiction or prevent mootness. Must explain why an order is needed and time-sensitive, attach stamped copies of the order and any notice of appeal, show prior service, and include the filing fee if the case is not yet docketed.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 40(b); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 26(3)"},{"t":"Frivolous-appeal penalties","d":"Court of Appeals: up to $10,000 against a party or counsel in a civil case, entered as a money judgment. Supreme Court: up to $2,500, plus penalties for appeals taken for delay under O.C.G.A. § 5-6-6. Discourteous personal remarks about judges or counsel are forbidden.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 7(e), 10; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 6, 7"}]],["Filing, fees, and service",[{"t":"Mandatory e-filing; filing times","d":"Attorneys must e-file through eFast (Court of Appeals) or SCED (Supreme Court) unless exempted. E-filings count as filed through 11:59 p.m. if they comply with the rules, except Court of Appeals reconsideration motions (4:30 p.m.). Noncompliant filings are rejected and count as filed only when resubmitted in compliance. Keep eFast/SCED contact information current; failure to receive notice is no excuse.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 4(d), 9(e), 46; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 4(7), 4(12), 13"},{"t":"Fees","d":"$300 in civil cases and $80 in criminal cases, due with the application or, in direct appeals, with the appellant’s brief; the clerk will not accept the filing without it (or an approved indigence affidavit). No additional fee for the brief after a granted application. Court of Appeals admission: $30 (special expedited admission $200 extra).","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 5, 9(a)–(b); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 4(2), 5"},{"t":"Service is separate from e-filing","d":"Serve by mail, personal service, or email, and say which in the certificate of service. Email service requires a prior agreement and a specific certification, and the certificate must list each recipient’s full name, email, phone, and mailing address. Filing in the e-filing system is not service. Documents without a proper certificate are not accepted.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 6; Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 14"}]],["Precedent and decisions",[{"t":"What counts as binding Court of Appeals precedent","d":"Since August 1, 2020, a published opinion in which a majority of the judges fully concur is binding. Earlier division opinions bind only if all three judges fully concurred; otherwise they are “physical precedent” (persuasive only) and must be marked as such when cited. Unreported decisions and Rule 36 affirmances without opinion are not precedent. A division can overrule precedent only unanimously after consulting the full court; otherwise en banc.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 33.2, 33.3, 36"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (direct appeal)","d":"30 days from entry of the appealable judgment or order.\nNot verified from collected text; confirm in the code, including tolling by motion for new trial, motion in arrest of judgment, or motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict.","c":"O.C.G.A. § 5-6-38 (statute not collected)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the appealable judgment or order)","note":"Not verified from collected text; confirm in the code, including tolling by motion for new trial, motion in arrest of judgment, or motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict."}},{"t":"Extension of notice-of-appeal deadline","d":"\nOnly one extension, not longer than the original period; try the trial court first.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 16(a); O.C.G.A. § 5-6-39"},{"t":"Interlocutory application","d":"10 days from entry of the order granting the certificate of immediate review.\nNo extensions. The time for the trial court to sign the certificate is in O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34(b) (not collected).","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(a); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order granting the certificate of immediate review)","note":"No extensions. The time for the trial court to sign the certificate is in O.C.G.A. § 5-6-34(b) (not collected)."}},{"t":"Response to interlocutory or discretionary application","d":"10 days from docketing of the application.\nOptional unless ordered; no extensions in the Court of Appeals.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(i), 31(i); Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 30, 33","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (docketing of the application)","note":"Optional unless ordered; no extensions in the Court of Appeals."}},{"t":"Discretionary application","d":"30 days from entry (filing) of the trial court order.\n7 days for dispossessory orders (O.C.G.A. § 44-7-56). Extension only by motion filed on or before the due date.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 31(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry)","note":"7 days for dispossessory orders (O.C.G.A. § 44-7-56). Extension only by motion filed on or before the due date."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after an application is granted","d":"10 days from order granting the application.\nFile in the trial court.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 30(j), 31(j)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (order granting the application)","note":"File in the trial court."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief (Court of Appeals)","d":"20 days from docketing.\nExtension motion must be filed before the due date.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 23(a)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (docketing)","note":"Extension motion must be filed before the due date."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief (Court of Appeals)","d":"40 days from docketing, or 20 days after appellant’s brief if later.\nRequired of the State in criminal appeals.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 23(b)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (docketing, or 20 days after appellant’s brief if later)","note":"Required of the State in criminal appeals."}},{"t":"Reply brief (Court of Appeals)","d":"20 days from filing of appellee’s brief.\nOne reply only; 4,200 words.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 23(c)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of appellee’s brief)","note":"One reply only; 4,200 words."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief (Supreme Court)","d":"20 days from docketing (or order granting certiorari).\nSame for cross-appellant from cross-appeal docketing.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 10(1)(a), 45","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (docketing)","note":"Same for cross-appellant from cross-appeal docketing."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief (Supreme Court)","d":"40 days from docketing, or 20 days after appellant’s brief if later.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 10(1)(b)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (docketing, or 20 days after appellant’s brief if later)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief (Supreme Court)","d":"50 days from docketing, or 10 days after the response brief if later.\n7,000 words.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 10(2)","due":{"n":50,"from":"the trigger date (docketing, or 10 days after the response brief if later)","note":"7,000 words."}},{"t":"Oral argument request (Court of Appeals)","d":"20 days from docketing.\nNot extended by a brief extension.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 28(a)(2)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (docketing)","note":"Not extended by a brief extension."}},{"t":"Oral argument request (Supreme Court, appellant)","d":"20 days from docketing.\nAppellee: within 10 days after appellant’s brief is filed. Renew within 5 days after a transfer.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 51(1)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (docketing)","note":"Appellee: within 10 days after appellant’s brief is filed. Renew within 5 days after a transfer."}},{"t":"Motion for reconsideration (Court of Appeals)","d":"10 days from judgment or dismissal.\nMust be received by 4:30 p.m.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 37(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (judgment or dismissal)","note":"Must be received by 4:30 p.m."}},{"t":"Motion for reconsideration (Supreme Court)","d":"10 days from the ruling.\nMust be received or e-filed within the 10 days; no mailbox rule.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 13(3), 27(2)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (the ruling)","note":"Must be received or e-filed within the 10 days; no mailbox rule."}},{"t":"Notice of intent to apply for certiorari (filed in Court of Appeals)","d":"10 days from judgment, or order ruling on reconsideration.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 38(a)(1)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (judgment, or order ruling on reconsideration)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for certiorari (filed in Supreme Court)","d":"20 days from judgment, or disposition of reconsideration motion.\nFile notice of the petition in the Court of Appeals the same day.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 38(2)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (judgment, or disposition of reconsideration motion)","note":"File notice of the petition in the Court of Appeals the same day."}},{"t":"Response to certiorari petition","d":"20 days from filing of the petition.\nReply within 10 days after the response (Rule 42.1).","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 42","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the petition)","note":"Reply within 10 days after the response (Rule 42.1)."}},{"t":"Notice of intent to seek U.S. Supreme Court certiorari (filed in Court of Appeals)","d":"20 days from supreme Court of Georgia’s denial of certiorari.\nGenerally does not stay the remittitur.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 38(b)(1)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (supreme Court of Georgia’s denial of certiorari)","note":"Generally does not stay the remittitur."}},{"t":"Recusal motion (Court of Appeals)","d":"20 days from docketing.\nUnless good cause shown.","c":"Ga. Ct. App. R. 44(a)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (docketing)","note":"Unless good cause shown."}},{"t":"Recusal motion (Supreme Court)","d":"5 days from learning of the grounds.\nAnd no later than 10 days before oral argument.","c":"Ga. Sup. Ct. R. 26(2)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (learning of the grounds)","note":"And no later than 10 days before oral argument."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.gasupreme.us/"]]},"rulesName":"Uniform Rules for the Superior Courts of Georgia","rulesSrc":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"NOTE","t":"What this file covers","s":"Georgia’s civil procedure rules are the Civil Practice Act, a statute (O.C.G.A. § 9-11-1 et seq.), not court rules. No complete official free text of the Act was available (the state’s official code is hosted only by LexisNexis), so this file instead summarizes the Uniform Superior Court Rules (USCR), the statewide court rules that govern practice under the Act in superior courts, from the compilation dated November 5, 2026. Criminal, administrative, and judicial-administration rules are included briefly for completeness. Official legislative summaries show 2025–2026 amendments to O.C.G.A. §§ 9-11-12, 9-11-26, 9-11-28, 9-11-30, 9-11-41, 9-11-63 that are not reflected here.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"1","t":"Preamble","s":"The USCR are adopted under the courts’ inherent power and Ga. Const. Art. VI, Sec. IX, Par. I to secure speedy, efficient, and inexpensive resolution of cases. They yield to the Constitution and substantive law in any conflict and prevail over local practices statewide.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"1.1","t":"Repeal of local rules","s":"All local rules, internal operating procedures, and experimental rules of the superior courts expired December 31, 2010.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"1.2","t":"Authority to deviate; standing orders and pilot projects","s":"No deviation from the USCR is allowed. Courts may keep standing orders on internal processes that do not materially affect parties’ rights (case management, ADR programs, specialty courts, and similar), may adopt jury-excuse guidelines under O.C.G.A. § 15-12-10, and may run Supreme Court-approved pilot projects for one year (extendable one more). Standing orders on matters the USCR do not address (for example, ADR, e-filing, registry payments) bind a party only with actual notice, which courthouse posting alone does not give.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"1.3","t":"Repeal of earlier rules","s":"The old “Rules of the Superior Court” in former Ga. Code Ann. §§ 24-3301 to 24-3389 are repealed.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"1.4","t":"Matters of statewide concern","s":"The USCR apply statewide.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"1.5","t":"Deviation","s":"No local deviation. A judge may supersede a specific rule in a specific case only by an order explaining why, served on the attorneys.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"1.6","t":"Amendments","s":"A Council of Superior Court Judges committee recommends amendments to the Supreme Court; the State Bar and other uniform-rules committees get notice and a chance to comment.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"1.7","t":"Publication","s":"Rules and amendments are published in the Georgia Reports advance sheets and, unless stated otherwise, take effect on publication.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"Definitions","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 2.1–2.6.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"2.1","t":"Attorney","s":"Includes anyone admitted to practice in the superior courts, anyone otherwise permitted by law to represent a party, and any pro se party.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"2.2–2.5","t":"Judge, clerk, calendar clerk, assigned judge","s":"“Judge” includes senior judges authorized to serve; “clerk” includes deputies; the calendar clerk schedules hearings and trials under the assigned judge’s supervision; the “assigned judge” is the judge to whom the case is assigned (or, in general-calendar circuits, the judge responsible at the time).","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"2.6","t":"Filing","s":"“Filing” includes paper or electronic submission to the clerk; e-filing is governed by Rule 36.16.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"3.1","t":"Method of assignment","s":"In multi-judge circuits the clerk assigns cases under a judge-approved plan that equalizes caseloads. The clerk has no discretion, may not reveal the assigned judge before filing when timing matters, and no one may try to influence assignment.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"3.2","t":"Companion and related actions","s":"Actions with substantially the same parties, subject matter, or facts, refiled actions, and derivative or companion actions go to the judge who has (or had) the original action, generally the one with the lower case number.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"3.3","t":"Exclusive control; transfer to another judge","s":"The assigned judge has exclusive control of the case; reassignment requires that judge’s written order (and the receiving judge’s consent). A judge who knows of facts requiring recusal under Rule 25 may not transfer the case instead.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"3.4","t":"Local authority","s":"Each multi-judge circuit sets its assignment method and publishes it to the local bar, the clerk, and the Supreme Court.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Ex parte communications","s":"Judges may not initiate or consider ex parte communications about pending or impending proceedings except as allowed by law or rule.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.2","t":"Entry of appearance and pleadings","s":"An attorney may not appear until filing a signed entry of appearance or signed pleading stating the case style and number, the party represented, and the attorney’s name, State Bar number, address, phone, fax, and State-Bar-registered email. Within 48 hours after being retained, mail the entry to the court and opposing counsel or file it (late filing does not bar the appearance). An entry of appearance is not an answer. Appearing while not in good standing with the State Bar is punishable by contempt.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.3","t":"Withdrawal","s":"Withdrawal requires a written request to the judge stating that the client received written notice of intent to withdraw and 20 days passed without objection, or that the client consents. The notice must include the items listed in 4.3(2)(A)–(I) (court keeps jurisdiction, client’s duties, scheduled dates, corporate-representation warning, the right to object within 20 days and the exact 20th day), and counsel files a notification certificate. The request is granted unless made after a pretrial order or it would delay a published trial, disrupt the court, or be manifestly unfair. Substitution may instead be made by a notice signed by the party and new counsel, served on former counsel, opposing parties, and the judge (amended Nov. 5, 2026).","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.4","t":"Admission pro hac vice","s":"An eligible out-of-state (Domestic) or foreign lawyer files a verified application with the court, served on all appearing parties and the State Bar’s Office of General Counsel, including the Appendix A information and the name of an active Georgia sponsor who will appear of record. Fees: $75 per application and $200 per year (due January 15 each later year), payable to the State Bar, waived for qualifying pro bono and indigent-defense work. Applications are ordinarily granted unless admission would harm the administration of justice or other parties, risk inadequate representation, reflect regular practice in Georgia, or the applicant previously appeared without approval. The Georgia lawyer remains responsible, and out-of-state counsel’s conflicts do not delay any deadline or setting.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.5","t":"Firm appearances and withdrawals","s":"An appearance or withdrawal by one lawyer in a firm or professional corporation covers the other lawyers in that firm.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.6","t":"Notice of representation","s":"New counsel must promptly notify the calendar clerk (and opposing counsel in civil cases; the district attorney in criminal cases) in writing of the representation and of any later change of representation or contact information.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.7","t":"Use the assigned judge","s":"Do not present a matter in a case assigned to another judge except under the most compelling circumstances, and then disclose the assignment and inform the assigned judge promptly.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.8","t":"Notice of related cases","s":"Counsel must immediately tell the judges involved about any related action pending or previously pending before a judge in the same circuit.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.9","t":"Prior presentation to another judge","s":"Before presenting a matter already presented to another judge, disclose that fact and the result.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.10","t":"Settlements and dismissals","s":"Notify the assigned judge (and calendar clerk where appropriate) immediately upon settlement or dismissal of a civil action.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.11","t":"Duty to attend and remain","s":"Subject to Rule 17, attorneys with calendared matters must be in court at the call and stay until excused, contacting the court daily during an ongoing calendar; failure is punishable by contempt.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.12","t":"Binding authority","s":"Attorneys of record have apparent authority to make agreements for their clients in civil actions, and oral agreements, if established, are enforceable.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"4.13","t":"Limited appearances","s":"Limited-scope representation requires first filing a separate “Limited Appearance” entry (complying with Rule 4.2) stating its purpose and duration, and, within 5 days after it ends, a notice that it has ended served on the client, opposing counsel or parties, and the judge. Otherwise counsel remains of record until withdrawal under Rule 4.3.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Prompt completion of discovery","s":"To use the court’s compulsory process, discovery must be commenced promptly and completed within 6 months after the answer is filed (or, if no answer within 30 days of service or any extended date, within 6 months starting 30 days after service). The court may open, extend, reopen, or shorten the period at any time. Federal practice has no comparable rule cutting off compulsory process at a fixed period after the answer.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"5.2","t":"Filing discovery","s":"Depositions and original discovery are not filed unless O.C.G.A. § 9-11-29.1(a)(1)–(5) requires it. A party serving interrogatories, document requests, admission requests, or responses files only a certificate identifying what was served, when, and on whom.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"5.3","t":"Deposition duration","s":"Unless the court orders or the parties stipulate otherwise, a deposition is limited to one day of seven hours; the court must allow more time if needed for a fair examination or if the deponent or others cause delay. Mirrors FRCP 30(d)(1).","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"5.4","t":"Early planning conference and discovery plan","s":"The parties may agree to, the judge may order, or a party may petition for an early planning discovery conference (in person, phone, or video) to discuss claims, settlement, preservation, and a discovery plan. An agreed plan (covering issues, schedule, ESI, preservation, production format, inaccessible sources) or a joint report of disagreements may be submitted within 14 days of the meeting, by plaintiff’s counsel unless agreed otherwise. Unlike FRCP 26(f), the conference is not mandatory in every case.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"5.5","t":"Privilege","s":"A party withholding information as privileged or work product must expressly claim it and describe what is withheld well enough for others to assess the claim (a privilege log in substance, like FRCP 26(b)(5)(A)). After inadvertent production, the producing party may give notice; the receiving party must return, sequester, or destroy it, not use it until resolved, retrieve copies it disclosed, and may submit it for in camera review.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"6.1","t":"Filing motions","s":"Every pre-trial civil motion (unless consented to by all parties) must include citations of authority and, for unstipulated facts, affidavits or record citations. When a motion or response is e-filed, the filer must email the opposing parties and the assigned judge or designee contemporaneously and no later than 24 hours after e-filing.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"6.2","t":"Responses to motions","s":"Unless the judge orders otherwise or law provides, a party opposing a motion must serve and file its response, with authorities and affidavits or record citations for unstipulated facts, within 30 days after service of the motion. (State courts have their own Rule 6.2.)","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"6.3","t":"Hearings","s":"Civil motions, including summary judgment, are decided without oral hearing unless the court orders otherwise, except motions for new trial and for judgment notwithstanding the verdict. Oral argument on summary judgment is allowed on a separate “Request for Oral Hearing” filed with the motion or within 5 days after the response time expires.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"6.4","t":"Motions to compel","s":"A motion to compel under O.C.G.A. § 9-11-37 must quote or attach each disputed request, state the objection or response claimed insufficient and its grounds, and give reasons. Before filing any discovery motion (including to quash or for a protective order) counsel must confer in good faith and file a certificate that the conference occurred and failed. Objections must be specific, not general.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"6.5","t":"Summary judgment statements","s":"A summary-judgment motion under the Civil Practice Act must attach a separate, short statement of each theory of recovery and each material fact the movant says is undisputed; the response must include a separate statement of each material fact claimed to be genuinely disputed. Comparable in function to the separate statements many federal local rules require; FRCP 56 itself requires record citations instead.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"6.6","t":"Timing of summary judgment motions","s":"File early enough not to delay trial; no trial will be continued because a summary-judgment motion was filed late. (The Act’s own timing provisions, O.C.G.A. § 9-11-56, were not collected.)","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"6.7","t":"Emergency motions","s":"On written notice and good cause, the assigned judge may shorten or waive time limits for emergency motions (but not summary judgment) or hear them immediately; the motion must explain in detail why expedition is needed.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"7.1","t":"Civil pretrial conferences","s":"The judge may set a pretrial conference on motion or sua sponte, considering O.C.G.A. § 9-11-16 issues. The attorneys who will try the case must attend (another attorney of record only with consent and full authority), and each side presents a written proposed pretrial order. Failure to attend or submit one allows removal from the trial calendar, entry of the court’s own order, or other sanctions short of dismissal with prejudice.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"7.2","t":"Civil pretrial order form","s":"The proposed pretrial order follows the prescribed form: trial counsel, time estimate, pending motions, discovery completed, contentions, jury issues, specifications of negligence, contract terms, damages, stipulations, exhibit lists (authenticity stipulated unless noted), witness will-call and may-call lists, deposition designations, verdict forms, and settlement. Once entered it supersedes the pleadings, which may then be amended only to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"7.3","t":"Interpreters","s":"A party must file notice of the need for a qualified interpreter within a reasonable time, at least 5 days before the proceeding where practicable, naming the participants, length, and language. The court appoints a licensed interpreter at its expense; late notice can result in costs, and cancellations require 24 hours’ notice or the party may bear the interpreter’s expenses.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"7.4","t":"Criminal pretrial conferences","s":"In criminal cases, pending motions, special pleas, and demurrers not called to the judge’s attention at the pretrial conference are deemed abandoned unless the judge allows later hearing for good cause.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"8.1","t":"Scheduling trials","s":"The assigned judge alone sets hearings and trials and publishes calendars, and is responsible for keeping cases from languishing.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"8.2","t":"Ready list","s":"Cases ready under O.C.G.A. § 9-11-40 go on a ready list, placed there by the judge on notice or by a party after the pretrial order, generally in filing-date order with statutory priorities.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"8.3","t":"Trial calendar","s":"The calendar clerk publishes the trial calendar from the ready list at least 20 days before the term, and may email it to attorneys at their State-Bar-registered addresses (pro se parties by regular mail).","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"8.4","t":"Trial date","s":"Parties in the first 10 cases on a published calendar must appear ready on the stated date; others must be ready but may arrange a specific time or await a call on reasonable notice.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"8.5","t":"Continuances","s":"Continuances are not granted merely by agreement of counsel. A first continuance motion made within 5 days of the trial week without statutory grounds or good cause can bring a penalty of up to $50.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"8.6","t":"Special settings","s":"Special settings for jury trial are not favored.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"9.1","t":"Virtual proceedings: scope","s":"Any proceeding may be virtual with the parties’ consent and the court’s agreement. Without consent, all civil matters except trials may be virtual, including depositions, defaults, damages hearings, motions, and pre- and post-trial motions; in criminal cases most non-trial matters may be virtual unless law requires in-person proceedings. Effective for events on or after March 1, 2023.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"9.2","t":"Virtual proceedings: conduct","s":"The judge may set logistics and allocate costs; privileged attorney-client communication must be protected; records are made as for in-person proceedings. Witnesses may testify by video in civil matters at the judge’s discretion; in criminal matters an objection to video testimony is sustained but operates as a continuance motion. Virtual events that would be open court must be publicly accessible, with notice and a chance to object.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"10.1","t":"Voir dire","s":"Questions under O.C.G.A. § 15-12-133 are allowed, but form, time, and number are within the court’s discretion; hypotheticals are discouraged, and questions seeking prejudgment or legal opinions are improper. Objections to voir dire procedure must be raised promptly or are waived.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"10.2","t":"Opening statements","s":"All parties may open, limited to expected proof by admissible evidence or its lack; the court may limit time (amended Nov. 5, 2026). In criminal cases the defense may open after the State’s opening or after the State’s evidence.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"10.3","t":"Requests to charge","s":"Requests to charge must be numbered consecutively on separate sheets and submitted in duplicate at the start of trial (unless the pretrial order says otherwise); additional requests may cover unanticipated points.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"10.4","t":"Excusals from courtroom","s":"Only the judge may excuse a party, witness, or counsel from the courtroom during a proceeding.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Selection of juries","s":"After voir dire, parties may request 15 minutes to prepare for selection; after warning, the court may limit each peremptory challenge to not less than 1 minute, and an unexercised challenge is forfeited.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Voluntary dismissal after jury empaneled","s":"If a civil action is voluntarily dismissed after the jury is empaneled (other than by a final settlement dictated into the record), all court costs, including juror fees for every panel used, are taxed to the dismissing party. (Statutory voluntary-dismissal rules in O.C.G.A. § 9-11-41 were amended in 2025 and were not collected.)","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"13.1","t":"Argument time limits","s":"Closing arguments: 2 hours per side in civil cases (30 minutes in appeals from magistrate court); criminal limits range from 30 minutes (misdemeanor) to 2 hours (death or life cases).","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"13.2–13.4","t":"Extensions, number of arguments, conclusion","s":"Extensions must be sought, with reasons, before arguments begin. No more than two attorneys argue per party without leave, and only one in conclusion. In civil cases the plaintiff opens and concludes, unless the defendant introduces no evidence or admits a prima facie case.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Dismissal for failure to respond to call","s":"The court may dismiss a civil action, or a party’s pleading, without prejudice for failure to respond properly to the call of the case, and may hold an attorney in contempt for failing to appear without legal excuse.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Default judgments","s":"A party seeking default judgment must attach to the proposed judgment a written certificate stating the date and type of service, whether proof of service was filed within 5 business days of service (or when), that no defensive pleading is of record, and the defendant’s military status if required.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"15.1","t":"Garnishments","s":"Under O.C.G.A. § 18-4-61(5), the clerk may supervise initiation of garnishments and accept the affidavit if it is on personal knowledge, complete, and on proper forms; questionable matters go to the judge.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"16.1","t":"Leave of absence, 30 days or less","s":"An attorney may protect cases not on a published calendar or noticed for hearing by filing with the clerk, at least 30 days before the leave, a notice listing the cases, reason, and duration, copied to the judge and opposing counsel. It stands granted unless opposing counsel objects or the court denies it within ten days.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"16.2","t":"Longer or late leaves","s":"Leaves over 30 cumulative days, for cases already calendared or noticed, or not meeting Rule 16.1 timing require a written application filed and served at least ten days before submission to the judge (waivable by written consent); the court has discretion.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"16.3–16.4","t":"Effect and denial of leave","s":"A granted leave relieves counsel of trials, hearings, and depositions in the listed matters, but does not extend any deadline set by law or the court; a new request is needed if counsel later files a criminal speedy-trial demand. Nonconforming applications are denied; the rule includes a notice form.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"17.1","t":"Conflicts between courts","s":"A conflict exists only if the attorney is lead counsel (or subpoenaed) in two or more matters and certifies others cannot handle them. Give written notice, ideally received at least seven days before the conflict, to opposing counsel, each clerk, and each judge, with a proposed resolution. Priority: felony criminal, JQC, and habeas over civil (speedy-trial demands first); jury over non-jury; among non-jury, dependency and termination hearings, then trials, then other matters (including appellate arguments); otherwise the first-filed case (amended April 9, 2026).","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"17.2","t":"Attorneys serving as part-time judges","s":"Civil judges should promptly resolve conflicts from an attorney’s service as a part-time judge, mindful of juvenile time limits, but should not continue civil matters involving child safety or temporary support for that reason.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"18.1–18.8","t":"Senior judges","s":"Procedures for chief judges to request assistance from other judges and senior judges (disqualification, disability, or caseload), certificates of need, emergency appointments, residency, and senior judges’ election to practice law instead of serving.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"19.1","t":"Civil transfer for lack of jurisdiction or venue","s":"A timely motion that jurisdiction is lacking or venue improper (or the court’s own finding of no subject-matter jurisdiction) is treated as a motion to transfer to a proper Georgia court; the movant names that court. Serve written motions at least 10 days before hearing; opponents respond in writing within 10 days of service. The transfer order states the action is dismissed without prejudice unless the plaintiff pays accrued costs within 20 days of the cost bill; the court may award fees and taxes $50 transfer costs. The case continues in the transferee court without new service.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"19.2–19.3","t":"Criminal transfers; election contests","s":"The judge granting a criminal venue change continues to preside. Election-contest venue may move to any county, with costs borne by the transferor county.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"19.4","t":"Civil removal to defendant’s home county","s":"Subject to O.C.G.A. § 14-2-510(b)(4), a tort defendant may file a notice of removal within 45 days after service of the summons, moving the case to the county of its principal place of business; the defendant must pay transfer costs within 20 days of the cost bill or the removal stands dismissed. The plaintiff may move to remand within 45 days after notice from the receiving clerk; prior orders may be reviewed on motion within 15 days after the remand ruling.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Peremptory calendar","s":"The judge may publish a list of civil cases with expired discovery, requiring parties to announce readiness; failure to appear or respond can lead to dismissal without prejudice of the plaintiff’s action or the defendant’s answer, counterclaim, or cross-claim.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Limitation of access to court files","s":"All court records are public unless access is limited by law or this procedure (amended Nov. 5, 2026).","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"21.1–21.3","t":"Sealing motions, finding of harm, ex parte orders","s":"On motion or sua sponte the court may limit access by an order specifying the part, nature, duration, and reason; any party or nonparty opposing may request a hearing within 30 days. Sealing requires a finding that privacy harm clearly outweighs the public interest. Temporary ex parte limitation only under compelling circumstances.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"21.4–21.5","t":"Review and amendment of sealing orders","s":"Sealing orders are reviewable by interlocutory application to the appellate court, and may be amended on notice by the issuing court or appellate court; a hearing within 30 days must be held if requested in a motion to amend or vacate.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"21.6","t":"Redaction and filing under seal","s":"Protected identifiers are as defined in O.C.G.A. § 9-11-7.1. To file under seal, first file a redacted public version, then submit the unredacted version and a proposed sealing order directly to the court. Protective-order identifiers go on a separate page the clerk seals.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Electronic devices and recording of proceedings","s":"Governs phones and devices in courtrooms and requests to record proceedings. Recording requests should be made at least 24 hours ahead where practicable and generally approved; denial or limitation requires specific findings on listed factors, and objections not raised before the proceeding are waived.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"22.1","t":"Audiovisual media used in trials","s":"Media exhibits should be tendered in commonly playable formats on a clean USB drive with any needed player or codec; the judge may require advance delivery.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Withdrawal of funds from court registry","s":"An order paying out registry funds (except garnishments) must be accompanied by counsel’s certificate that all claimants of record consent in writing (and, in condemnation, that taxes and assessments are provided for).","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"24.1","t":"Domestic relations: scope","s":"Covers divorce, alimony, equitable division, custody, support, legitimation, paternity, related adoption and termination matters, contempt and modification, Family Violence Act actions, and foreign support judgments.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"24.2–24.2A","t":"Financial affidavits and worksheets","s":"At least 5 days before any temporary or final hearing on support, alimony, property division, or fees, each party serves a financial affidavit in the prescribed form, plus Child Support Commission worksheets and schedules in support cases. Monthly figures use 4.35 weeks per month and 174 hours per month.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"24.3–24.5","t":"Acknowledgments, notice, and witnesses","s":"Acknowledgments of service and consents must be witnessed by an attesting officer or counsel. At temporary hearings each side may present the parties and one other live witness; other testimony is by deposition or affidavit served at least 24 hours before. Children do not testify at temporary hearings without leave.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"24.6–24.7","t":"Uncontested and contested divorce","s":"By written consent a divorce may be granted 31 days after service or acknowledgment; if unanswered, 46 days after service; if served by publication, 61 days after first publication. No decree issues in a contested case until all contestable issues are resolved.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"24.8–24.12","t":"Parenting seminars, GALs, parenting plans, income withholding","s":"Circuits may require a seminar of up to four hours on divorce’s effects on children. Rules govern guardian ad litem appointment and duties, mandatory parenting plans (O.C.G.A. § 19-9-1(b) content, in the prescribed form) in custody cases, and a separate income withholding order in child-support cases unless an exception applies.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"25.1","t":"Recusal motions: timing","s":"Motions to recuse must be written, supported by affidavits, and filed and presented no later than 5 days after learning of the grounds and 10 days before the hearing or trial at issue, unless good cause is shown; they may not delay the proceeding. Georgia has no peremptory challenge to a judge in these rules.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"25.2","t":"Affidavit","s":"The affidavit must state specific facts (time, place, persons, circumstances) of extra-judicial conduct showing bias or prejudice; conclusions and opinions are insufficient.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"25.3","t":"Duty of the trial judge","s":"The challenged judge stops acting on the merits and immediately decides only timeliness and legal sufficiency, assuming the facts true; if recusal would be authorized, another judge hears the motion, the allegations stand denied, and the challenged judge may not otherwise oppose (amended April 9, 2026). Guided by Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"25.4–25.7","t":"Selection of judge; findings; voluntary recusal","s":"The hearing judge is chosen by the district administrative judge, the other judge in a two-judge circuit, or the circuit’s random method (never by the challenged judge). The assigned judge may rule on affidavits or hold a hearing and must make written findings. A voluntary recusal is not an admission.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"26–35","t":"Criminal procedure rules","s":"Rules 26–35 govern criminal matters: first appearance within 48 hours of a warrantless arrest (72 with a warrant) and commitment hearings (26), pretrial release programs (27), jail census (28), indigent-defense appointment (29), arraignment (30), criminal motions and notices due at least 10 days before trial for certain defenses and child-hearsay statements (31), criminal trial calendars (32), guilty pleas and plea agreements (33), the Unified Appeal in death-penalty cases (34), and post-sentence reporting (35). Not summarized further here.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"36.1–36.4","t":"Document preparation, docketing, caption, signatures","s":"Filings should be on 8½ x 11 white paper, one side, with a caption stating the exact nature of the pleading. Proposed orders and judgments must show the preparer’s name, bar number, address, phone, fax, and email; avoid separate signature pages, and any separate signature page must identify the parties, case number, and document.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"36.5–36.9","t":"Originals, minutes, transcripts, file categories, case numbers","s":"Originals stay with the clerk (attorneys may carry the file to the judge for a hearing). Case numbers follow the sequence year–type–number–judge.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"36.10–36.12","t":"Civil filing requirements","s":"Complaints are filed only with the filing fee and sheriff’s fee (or pauper’s affidavit), a civil case initiation form, service copies, and any required forms; dispositions require a civil case disposition form. Returns of service go on the clerk’s form. Advance costs are the minimum costs.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"36.13–36.15","t":"Criminal filing and costs","s":"Requirements for indictments, accusations, no bills, criminal-history data, and minimum criminal costs of $100.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"36.16","t":"Electronic filing","s":"E-filing is available when required by law or adopted under Judicial Council standards. Registered filers may e-file anything except sealed, in camera, or restricted documents (original depositions may be e-filed). An e-filed document is deemed signed by the filer, presumed filed when the provider receives and confirms it, and deemed served on parties who registered for e-service. Courts grant relief (nunc pro tunc filing or extensions) for system failures; clerks give notice and a chance to cure defective e-filings.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"36.17","t":"Sensitive information","s":"Under O.C.G.A. § 9-11-7.1, filings may include only the last four digits of Social Security, taxpayer ID, and financial account numbers and the year of birth. Counsel and parties alone are responsible for redaction; the clerk does not check.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"36.18","t":"Electronic signatures of judges","s":"Judges may sign orders electronically, using secure, auditable, non-editable methods with date, time, and case number where practicable.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"36.19","t":"Digital recording of proceedings","s":"Rules for digital recording systems authorized by O.C.G.A. §§ 5-6-41, 9-14-50, and 15-14-1: qualified digital monitors, storage, and transcripts prepared only by certified court reporters with a required certification (adopted Feb. 26, 2026). Does not apply to depositions.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Court administrators","s":"District and local court administrators perform administrative duties assigned by the courts.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Filing of remittitur and judgment","s":"On receiving an appellate remittitur, the clerk files the notice of appeal, remittitur, and index with the original action.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Docketing and indexing","s":"Clerks maintain civil, criminal, lis pendens, general execution, and adoption dockets and use the required civil case initiation and disposition forms.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Computer applications","s":"Computerized records must use the same data elements and security as manual systems.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"41.1–41.2","t":"Motions for new trial","s":"Motions for new trial must be heard and decided as promptly as possible. In criminal cases the court holds a status conference within 120 days after sentencing and about every 180 days after, and issues a show-cause notice to the reporter if the transcript is not filed within 120 days.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"41.3","t":"Transcript costs","s":"Except for pauper cases, an attorney who files a motion for new trial or a notice of appeal designating a transcript is personally responsible for the court reporter’s fee, and the filing certifies the transcript has been ordered; filing before ordering it can bring discipline.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"41.4","t":"Transmission of the record","s":"On a notice of appeal the clerk compiles and transmits the record under O.C.G.A. § 5-6-43; failure within 60 days of the statutory deadlines can bring a show-cause hearing for the clerk.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Special counsel","s":"Private special prosecutors retained by victims’ families may not participate in prosecutions; the court may appoint counsel to assist retained defense counsel.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Mandatory continuing judicial education","s":"Education requirements, administration, sanctions, and exemptions for superior court judges.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Habeas corpus in death-sentence cases","s":"Procedures for first state habeas petitions by death-sentenced petitioners (O.C.G.A. § 9-14-47.1): clerk service on the Council within ten days, judicial assignment, answer, scheduling, motions, discovery, hearing, transcript, briefing, and ruling.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Court security and emergency operations","s":"Each circuit must have a confidential courthouse security plan (sheriff with chief judge, reviewed annually) and a judicial emergency operations plan.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Special masters","s":"Unless a statute provides otherwise, the court may appoint a master on motion or sua sponte for consented duties, pretrial and post-trial matters, technical subjects such as e-discovery, compliance monitoring, accountings, out-of-state depositions, or (in exceptional conditions) non-jury fact-finding, after notice and a chance to be heard. A party may move to reject or modify a master’s order, report, or recommendations within 20 days after service (deemed received three days after mailing, same day if electronic or hand-delivered); otherwise it becomes the court’s order. Fact findings and legal conclusions are reviewed de novo unless the parties stipulate otherwise. Modeled on FRCP 53, which allows 21 days to object.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Uncontested agency adoptions","s":"The judge should hold the final hearing on an agency adoption (O.C.G.A. § 19-8-4) within fifteen days after counsel certifies all statutory requirements are met, and may hear it in any county of the circuit with consent.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Weapons carry license inquiry","s":"At sentencing for listed offenses and in other listed situations, the judge must ask whether the person holds a weapons carry license, and the clerk notifies the issuing probate court within 10 days.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Emergency dispossessory (CARES Act)","s":"For residential nonpayment dispossessories filed before August 25, 2020, landlords had to file a verification on CARES Act coverage and, for covered properties, give the 30-day federal notice. Historical; retained in the compilation.","u":"https://www.gasupreme.us/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/UNIFORM-SUPERIOR-COURT-RULES-2026_11_05.pdf"}]});
